index 0000000..9a2d6d5
--- /dev/null
+++ b/NOTES-engram-upstream.md
@@ -0,0 +1,120 @@
+# Porting upstream's engram work (branch `dsv41-engram-upstream`)
+
+Four upstream ideas were assessed after the vLLM/SGLang comparison showed that our
+largest "win" had been a regression of our own making. One was ported, three were
+rejected with measurements.
+
+## 1. Fused decode hash + history commit — PORTED
+
+Upstream: sgl-project/sglang **#39138**, merged 2026-09-12T00:00:26Z, 257+/22-.
+`engram_hash_ids_and_commit` writes the request's new n-gram history from inside the
+hash kernel, replacing the five torch ops that followed it in decode: the
+`req_pool_indices` cast, `out_cache_loc == 0`, `where`, `flip` and the `index_put`.
+Rows whose `out_cache_loc` is 0 are CUDA-graph padding and write nothing. Extend keeps
+the torch commit; target verify is unchanged and commits after acceptance.
+
+The two kernel files applied verbatim (`git apply -p2`, offsets only), which confirms
+our base matches the PR's base for them.
+
+**The call site could not be taken verbatim.** This tree hashes twice per decode step:
+once eagerly before graph replay to build the engram pre-gather (`commit=False`, see
+`deepseek_v4.py:engram_fill_decode_pregather`) and once inside the graph
+(`commit=True`), and the in-graph call owns the single history commit. Upstream's
+unconditional early return would have made the eager call commit too, advancing every
+request's history twice per step and corrupting the n-gram state silently. The fused
+path is therefore gated on `commit`, and sits before the `req_slots` cast so a fused
+decode step skips that op as well.
+
+Flag `SGLANG_DSV41_ENGRAM_FUSED_COMMIT`, default off, consistent with every other
+optimization in this tree: the patched tree with no flags set behaves exactly like the
+unpatched one.
+
+### Verification
+
+`kernels/jit/tests/test_dsv41_engram_fused_commit.py`, 21 cases, all passing. Each runs
+the old path (kernel + five torch ops) and the new one on identical inputs and requires
+equal hash ids and equal live history rows:
+
+| case | why |
+|---|---|
+| batch sizes 1, 2, 3, 5, 8, 17, 64 | program boundaries and tails |
+| padded row aliasing a live slot | the padded row must not overwrite a live commit |
+| 100 rows over four Triton programs, 10 aliased padded rows | cross-program read/write order |
+| positions 0–3 with n = 4 | blocked shifts at the start of a sequence |
+| zeroed history | the freshly-initialised state |
+| every row padded | history must not change at all |
+| CUDA graph capture and replay | replayed ids and history equal the eager result |
+| `EngramHasher.forward`, flag on vs off, bs 1/4/6/33 | nothing a caller observes changes |
+| `forward(commit=False)` with the flag on | the eager pre-replay call never writes |
+| target verify, flag on vs off | untouched, and still commits nothing in-forward |
+
+Hash ids are integers, so every comparison is bitwise. compute-sanitizer on the fused
+kernel across the aliasing cases: memcheck 0 errors, racecheck 0 hazards.
+
+The only intentional difference is the pad row: the old path writes it, the fused path
+skips it. Nothing reads it — padded rows' hash ids are discarded.
+
+Expected gain is small. Upstream measured 227.9 → 228.1 tok/s at bs=1 on 4×GB300
+(+0.1%); here it removes 5 of ~1708 decode graph nodes. It ships as a
+neutral-but-better change: bitwise output, fewer nodes, upstream-merged.
+
+## 2. Head-sharded lookup (vLLM) — REJECTED, arithmetic
+
+vLLM shards the engram table by hash head across TP ranks at load time
+(`ParallelEngramEmbedding`, `vllm/models/deepseek_v4_1/common/engram.py:623-700`):
+each rank holds `part_num_embeddings` rows and all-gathers ~3 KB after lookup.
+
+It buys us nothing:
+
+- **Host memory: no change.** Our pinned table is one memfd *shared by both ranks*, so
+  the node already holds one copy. Sharding would give each rank half the rows — the
+  same total.
+- **Gather traffic: halved, but the gather is not the cost.** The in-graph gather is
+  10.4 µs per engram layer, 20.8 µs of a 6810 µs step (0.3%), and at 72 random rows it
+  is latency-bound rather than bandwidth-bound, so halving the bytes does not halve the
+  time.
+- **It adds an all-gather per engram layer per step** on a PCIe pair with no NVLink,
+  which costs the same order as the saving.
+
+Rejected: an invasive re-layout of the pinned table and weight loader to trade ~5 µs of
+gather for a collective of similar cost.
+
+## 3. `MADV_COLLAPSE` after filling — REJECTED, measured
+
+The idea was to fill the memfd on base pages (fast) and then fold it into huge pages
+afterwards, recovering TLB reach without the direct-compaction stall that made
+`MADV_HUGEPAGE`-at-map-time unusable (0.04 GiB/s on the second table).
+
+Measured on this box in its serving state (4 GiB memfd probe, `shmem_enabled=[advise]`):
+
+| step | result |
+|---|---|
+| fill, base pages | 0.8 s, 4.95 GiB/s, 0 MiB huge |
+| `MADV_HUGEPAGE` after the fill | returns 0, instant |
+| `MADV_COLLAPSE` | **ENOMEM after 2.5 s, 0 MiB huge** |
+
+The kernel cannot find contiguous 2 MiB blocks: the box has ~190 GB free but it is
+fragmented, in large part by the 189 GiB pinned table itself. Extrapolated to the real
+188 GiB this is roughly two minutes of boot time for no huge pages at all.
+
+`_collapse()` already exists in this tree and is called only for the `private`
+(anonymous) layout, which is correct — shmem refuses under `advise`. Nothing to change.
+
+Note this measurement is state-dependent: on a freshly booted machine collapse might
+succeed. That is not the state our lanes run in.
+
+## 4. `background=True` grid halving — REJECTED, not applicable
+
+vLLM halves the lookup grid to leave SMs for concurrent work. Our gather is 0.3% of the
+decode step and the step is not SM-starved there; halving the grid would make the
+gather slower for no benefit.
+
+## Shipping
+
+`/home/ian/dsv41/ship_engram_upstream.sh` waits for the GPU lock (the GPQA control
+experiment holds it), then runs A/B/A on the 1M cc3 config with the full deployed flag
+set in every arm, plus GSM8K-100 no-think, GSM8K-20 thinking and an 800K-char needle on
+arm B. It ships only if the deltas clear the measured noise floor and the quality gates
+pass: merge into `dsv41-attn-longctx`, fast-forward the production tree, add the flag to
+both lane blocks in `config.yml`, and restart llama-swap while `/running` is empty.
+Results land in `/home/ian/dsv41/engram_upstream_ship.log`.
