index 0000000..b2d466a
--- /dev/null
+++ b/NOTES-flat-moe.md
@@ -0,0 +1,248 @@
+# SM120 flat persistent MXFP4 MoE for decode (branch `dsv41-flat-mxfp4-moe`)
+
+Replaces, behind `SGLANG_DSV41_FLAT_MOE_DECODE=1`, the FlashInfer CUTLASS grouped
+GEMM path for routed-expert calls with M <= `SGLANG_DSV41_FLAT_MOE_MAX_M` (default
+64) tokens: decode steps and DSpark verify batches. Larger calls (prefill chunks)
+and the streaming expert-cache path keep the CUTLASS path unchanged.
+
+Files: `kernels/jit/csrc/moe/sm120_flat_mxfp4_moe.cuh` (kernels + tvm-ffi host),
+`kernels/ops/moe/sm120_flat_mxfp4_moe.py` (JIT wrapper, workspace, prebuild),
+`srt/layers/quantization/mxfp4_flashinfer_cutlass_moe.py` (`_apply_flat`, prebuild at
+weight load, dispatch in `apply`), `srt/environ.py` (flags), tests under
+`kernels/jit/tests/`, benchmark `benchmark/bench_sm120_flat_mxfp4_moe.py`.
+
+## Layouts found (FlashInfer CUTLASS SM120 MXFP4, as stored on the layer)
+
+- `w13_weight`: uint8 `[E, 2I, H/2]`, one e2m1 nibble per element, low nibble =
+  even k (`fp4_quantization.py:138-141`); rows `[0, I)` are the **up** projection and
+  `[I, 2I)` the **gate** (`load_up_proj_weight_first`; TRT-LLM's gated activation reads
+  `linear = fc1[i]`, `gate = fc1[i + inter]` and returns `act(gate) * linear`,
+  `cutlass_fused_moe_kernels.cuh:2100-2108`).
+- `w2_weight`: uint8 `[E, H, I/2]`, same packing.
+- `w13_weight_scale_inv` / `w2_weight_scale_inv`: `float8_e8m0fnu` (raw ue8m0 bytes),
+  logical `[E, N, K/32]`, stored **swizzled in place** by `block_scale_interleave`
+  (128x4 layout). Byte offset of scale (n, kb) inside an expert:
+  `(kb & 3) + (kb >> 2) * 512 + (n & 31) * 16 + ((n & 127) >> 5) * 4 + (n >> 7) * 128 * (K/32)`
+  (`quantization_cute_dsl_utils.py:535-575`). The kernel reads this layout directly, so
+  no unswizzled copy is kept (an extra copy would cost ~5.3 GB per rank for 224 x 40
+  experts).
+- Activations: `mxfp8_quantize(x, is_sf_swizzled_layout=False)` gives e4m3 `[M, H]` and
+  ue8m0 `[M, H/32]` in row-major (linear) order; same quantizer and numerics as the
+  production path, only the scale arrangement differs.
+- Activation: V4.1 config `hidden_act=silu`, `swiglu_limit=10.0`. SGLang's DSv4
+  `silu_and_mul` clamps `gate <= limit`, `|up| <= limit` (in bf16) before SiLU
+  (`kernels/jit/csrc/deepseek_v4/silu_and_mul_masked_post_quant.cuh:53-68`). The
+  FlashInfer path is called with `ActivationType.Swiglu` plus a `swiglu_limit` tensor,
+  but `GLUAdaptor<SiLu>` ignores `limit` (`cutlass_fused_moe_kernels.cuh:2003-2014`),
+  so the production experts run **unclamped**. `SGLANG_DSV41_FLAT_MOE_CLAMP` (default
+  on) applies the model's clamp; off reproduces the production activation.
+- Routing weights already include `routed_scaling_factor` (`fuse_routed_scaling_factor_in_topk`);
+  `topk_ids` reaching `apply` are kept-slot ids after the prune remap. The shared
+  expert is computed outside the routed-expert method and is untouched.
+
+## Design as built
+
+Three launches per layer after the activation quantize (production path: 16-19):
+
+1. `build_jobs_kernel` (64 blocks x 256): block 0 groups the (token, slot) pairs by
+   expert into jobs of <= 16 rows (histogram + scans in shared memory, E <= 512,
+   pairs <= 512) and writes the job tables; all blocks zero the completion counters
+   this call touches (the partial-sum slots need no zeroing).
+2. `gateup_kernel` (persistent grid = SMs x resident blocks, 128 threads): work items
+   `(job, paired tile, K split)`; a paired tile is 32 up rows + the 32 matching gate
+   rows (warps 0-1 / 2-3), so the item that completes a `(job, tile)` holds both halves.
+   K split `BK1 = 1280` for H = 5120 (4 splits). Each split stores its fp32 partial
+   sums to its own slot `ws1[split][pair][2I]` (plain stores, no atomics on data); a
+   per-`(job, tile)` counter elects the last arriver, which reads the splits through
+   L2 (`__ldcg`) and sums them in fixed order, rounds to bf16 (the CUTLASS gemm1
+   output dtype), applies SwiGLU (+clamp), and quantizes the row's 32 values (exactly
+   one MXFP8 block) to e4m3 + ue8m0 for the down projection.
+3. `down_kernel`: items `(job, 64-row tile of H, K split)`, `BK2 = 576` for I = 1152
+   (2 splits). Each split stores `outp[split][pair][H]`; a per-`(token, tile)` counter
+   (target = valid pairs of the token x K splits) elects the last arriver, which
+   combines the token's pairs (`p = token * topk + slot`, fixed slot and split order)
+   with their routing weights and converts the 64-wide slice to bf16.
+
+No atomics on data anywhere, so the output is bitwise reproducible run to run and
+across processes; only the completion counters are atomic (the winner's identity
+does not affect the summation order). A first version used fp32 atomics; its
+run-to-run jitter (~0.2% after the MXFP8 re-quantization amplified ulp-level
+ordering differences) was the reason for the change.
+
+Streaming item (shared by 2 and 3): the 16 A rows (e4m3) and their scales are staged in
+shared memory once per item; the 64 weight rows stream through a 3-stage `cp.async.cg`
+ring (128-k chunks for gate/up, 64-k chunks for down), each stage issued as 16-byte
+copies by all 128 threads before the previous stage is consumed. Each `(expert,
+tile, K split)` weight byte is read from HBM once per job; an expert routed to more
+than 16 tokens of one call is read once per 16-row job (L2 absorbs the repeat).
+
+Tensor core: `mma.sync.aligned.kind::mxf8f6f4.block_scale.scale_vec::1X.m16n8k32.row.col.f32.e4m3.e2m1.f32.ue8m0`
+(SASS `QMMA.SF.16832.F32.E4M3.E2M1.E8`), A = e4m3 activations, B = e2m1 weights in
+8-bit containers with the value in bits [5:2] (CUTLASS `fp4_shift`, `mma_traits_sm120.hpp:211-217`),
+fp32 accumulate, both ue8m0 scales applied by the hardware. Scale-factor thread
+mapping as in DeepGEMM's sm120 kernels (`mma/sm120.cuh`, `sm120_fp4_mqa_logits.cuh:249-286`):
+immediates `{0,0}`, thread `lane%4 == t` supplies SF_A for row `lane/4 + (t&1)*8`, SF_B
+for column `lane/4`. Within each 32-wide scale block the k order is permuted identically
+for A and B (thread t owns k = 8t..8t+7), so one 4-byte load of packed nibbles gives
+both B fragments and one 8-byte load both A fragments; no `ldmatrix`, no transposes.
+Shared-memory row pads (W: +16 B, A: +32 B) make the fragment reads bank-conflict-free.
+
+PDL: every kernel starts with `griddepcontrol.launch_dependents` then
+`griddepcontrol.wait` before its first dependent read; launches carry
+`cudaLaunchAttributeProgrammaticStreamSerialization` when `SGLANG_DSV41_FLAT_MOE_PDL`
+(default on). Correctness relies on the documented semantics of `griddepcontrol.wait`
+(all prerequisite grids completed and their memory flushed); the early trigger only
+lets the next grid's blocks be scheduled into SM slots freed by finishing blocks.
+Whether the prologues actually overlap was not measured (no nsys while the benchmark
+holds the GPUs); the CUDA-graph tests capture and replay with the attribute.
+
+## Verified vs assumed
+
+Verified on the device (RTX PRO 6000 Blackwell, 188 SMs, CUDA 13.0, `sm_120f`):
+the mixed e4m3 x e2m1 block-scaled MMA and `griddepcontrol` compile for both
+`sm_120a` and `sm_120f`; occupancy from `cudaOccupancyMaxActiveBlocksPerMultiprocessor`:
+gate/up 2 blocks/SM (40,000 B dynamic smem), down 4 blocks/SM (21,184 B) for the
+production shape; 2 / 6 for the small test shape. That is 100 KB of shared memory
+per SM, so at most two 40 KB items per SM: in flight per SM = 2 blocks x 3 stages x
+5 KB = 30 KB of weight loads (188 SMs -> 5.6 MB), well above the ~1 MB Little's-law
+requirement at 1.8 TB/s. The scale-factor mapping, container bit position, fragment
+k-permutation and the swizzle formula are verified by the numerical tests (random
+per-row scales would expose any mismatch). TMA / `cp.async.bulk` were not used:
+plain `cp.async.cg` 16-byte copies from all threads already saturate the request rate
+at this occupancy and keep the kernel free of tensor maps.
+
+Assumed (documented, not measured): the early PDL trigger placement; the exact ue8m0
+rounding of FlashInfer's activation kernel for the intermediate (the kernel rounds
+the block scale up like `cvt.rp.ue8m0`, giving `amax / scale <= 448`).
+
+## Numerics
+
+W4A8 like the production path (e4m3 activations, e2m1 weights, fp32 accumulate).
+Differences from the CUTLASS path: the intermediate MXFP8 quantization happens in the
+gate/up epilogue with the kernel's own rounding rule; the K-split partial sums are
+added in a fixed order (deterministic); the clamp is applied when enabled. Prefill (CUTLASS) and decode (this kernel) therefore differ slightly in
+numerics for the same weights, as already accepted for the dense GEMV.
+
+Test results (`test_sm120_flat_mxfp4_moe.py`, fp32 reference on dequantized operands,
+8 experts, seeds fixed), small shape H=1024 I=256, all cases M in {1,4,6,17,24,64},
+top-k in {3,6,8}, random and skewed (16-row jobs) routing, clamp on/off:
+
+| case | kernel max abs err / mean | ref max | FlashInfer CUTLASS max / mean (same inputs, unclamped) |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| M=1 unclamped | 0.12 / 0.021 | 61.0 | 1.06 / 0.170 |
+| M=24 unclamped | 0.25 / 0.021 | 112.8 | 3.57 / 0.203 |
+| M=64 skewed | 0.44 / 0.020 | 153.4 | 4.43 / 0.197 |
+| M=64 topk 8 | 0.25 / 0.025 | 124.7 | 3.60 / 0.259 |
+| clamped cases | <= 0.03 / 0.002 | 5-9 | n/a |
+
+The new kernel is ~10x closer to the fp32 reference than the production CUTLASS path
+on identical quantized inputs; the residual CUTLASS deviation is in its own activation
++ re-quantization stage (kernel-vs-FlashInfer distance equals FlashInfer-vs-reference).
+CUDA-graph replay (with and without PDL) reproduces eager bit for bit, and the
+SGLang-level test checks `apply()` against the direct kernel call bitwise.
+Production shape (H=5120, I=1152) results: see the "Production shape" section below.
+
+The first test run failed on every case with 5-10% errors: the pipeline's tail
+iteration consumed the last chunk before its `cp.async` group had landed
+(`wait_group<1>` with no new group committed). Fixed by committing an empty group on
+iterations that issue nothing.
+
+### Production shape (H=5120, I=1152 per rank, 8 experts)
+
+| case | kernel max abs err / mean | ref max | FlashInfer CUTLASS max / mean (same inputs, unclamped) |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| M=1 unclamped | 1.95 / 0.228 | 848 | 22.4 / 3.35 |
+| M=24 unclamped | 2.82 / 0.230 | 1141 | 32.7 / 2.77 |
+| M=64 skewed unclamped | 3.88 / 0.235 | 1230 | 28.0 / 2.58 |
+| clamped (limit 1.0) M=1/24/64 | <= 0.06 / 0.004 | 13-23 | n/a |
+
+CUDA-graph replay with PDL: bitwise equal to eager. SGLang-level test
+(`test_sm120_flat_mxfp4_moe_layer.py`, the real `Mxfp4FlashinferCutlassMoEMethod.apply`
+on a layer holding the production parameter dtypes, int64 topk ids, limit 10):
+`apply()` output bitwise equal to the direct kernel call for M = 1, 6, 24, 64; max err
+vs the fp32 reference 1.0-2.0 on a 540-580 scale (mean 0.11-0.14); M above
+`SGLANG_DSV41_FLAT_MOE_MAX_M` falls through to the CUTLASS path; capture + replay
+through `apply()` with PDL bitwise equal; a second process reproduces the numbers
+exactly (determinism).
+
+## Memory
+
+Per device, allocated once per (H, I) and shared by all layers: `ws1` fp32
+`[4, 512, 2I]` (18.9 MB), `outp` fp32 `[2, 512, H]` (21 MB), `hq` u8 `[512, I]`
+(0.6 MB), `hsf`, int32 tables (~0.2 MB): ~41 MB per rank. No per-layer weight copies.
+Only the completion counters are zeroed per call (proportional to M x topk); the
+partial slots need no zeroing because every slot an item reads is written by exactly
+one item.
+
+## Launch count and expected effect
+
+Per MoE layer at decode: activation quantize (FlashInfer) + 3 = 4 launches, against
+16-19 on the CUTLASS path (top-k remap 5, quantize, prologue, expand, gemm1, gemm2 +
+finalize, shared expert). The prune remap (5 tiny kernels) still runs before the
+kernel and is the next thing to fold (gate-row permutation, see the code review).
+
+## Benchmark procedure (not run: both GPUs are held by the Terminal-Bench run)
+
+```
+SRC=/home/ian/dsv41/sglang   # or the scratchpad tree
+docker run --rm --gpus '"device=0"' \
+  -v $SRC:/sgl-workspace/sglang/python/sglang:ro \
+  -v /mnt/nvme-1/dsv41-sglang-jit:/root/.cache/sglang local/sglang-dsv41:base \
+  python /sgl-workspace/sglang/python/sglang/benchmark/bench_sm120_flat_mxfp4_moe.py \
+    --m 1,4,6,12,24,48,64 --experts 224 --copies 8
+```
+It cycles 8 distinct 224-expert sets (16 GB) inside one CUDA graph so every call
+streams cold weights, and prints per-call time and effective HBM bandwidth against
+the bytes of the experts each call routes to, for the flat path and the CUTLASS path.
+Then the end-to-end check: boot a lane with `-e SGLANG_DSV41_FLAT_MOE_DECODE=1` (via
+`EXTRA_ENV` of `launch_prune.sh`), run `bench_v41.py` single-stream and cc4, and
+`gsm8k_eval.py`/`quality_ab.py` for quality parity. Expected from the exl3 study:
+MoE share from ~0.38 to ~0.65 TB/s, +40-50% single-stream decode, +20-30% at cc4.
+
+## Risks / not done
+
+- Not benchmarked; the occupancy and in-flight-bytes numbers are from the occupancy
+  API, not from a profile. If the gate/up kernel turns out latency-bound at 2 blocks/SM,
+  the next lever is 4 stages or a 32-row A tile (smaller smem) for 3 blocks/SM.
+- The illegal memory access seen in early test runs was a test bug: the CUDA-graph
+  check warmed up on a side stream without ordering it after the default stream, so
+  two calls raced on the shared workspace tables. SGLang issues the calls in stream
+  order; the tests now wait on the current stream. A `compute-sanitizer` pass when a
+  GPU is free is still worthwhile.
+- DSpark drafter layers (128 experts, keep-64) use the same code path when their
+  shapes satisfy `supports_flat_mxfp4_moe`; not separately tested.
+
+## In-situ measurement (2026-09-11, 1M cc3 config, keep224m, GEMV on; nsys `--cuda-graph-trace=node`, 13-19 decode steps at M=1)
+
+Same-boot, same-tool decode throughput (bench_v41.py 512 tokens; server-log gen throughput agrees):
+
+| path | 1 stream | 3 streams |
+|---|---|---|
+| CUTLASS (flag off) | 80-84 tok/s | 187-189 |
+| flat on, PDL on | 84-87 | 185-189 |
+| flat on, PDL off | 77 | 176 |
+
+Per decode step at M=1 (one TP rank, 40 MoE layers; step span 11.8 ms both ways, GPU busy 8.9 ms, ~2000 launches, 2.9 ms launch gaps):
+
+| per layer | CUTLASS path | flat path |
+|---|---|---|
+| grouped GEMM / gateup + down | 2 x 27.1 us = 54 us | 42.6 + 35.4 = 78 us |
+| prologue kernels / build_jobs | ~16 us (7 launches) | 6.6 us (1 launch) + 3.8 us quant |
+| total MoE | 70 us (2.79 ms/step) | 85 us (3.38 ms/step) |
+
+The flat kernels are not faster than the tuned CUTLASS grouped GEMM in situ: gateup streams w13 (37.6 MB) at 0.88 TB/s
+versus ~1.0 TB/s for the tuned CUTLASS gemm1, and down streams w2 (18.8 MB) at only 0.53 TB/s. The step time is
+unchanged because the flat path overlaps more with the alt streams (overlap 2.66 vs 1.98 ms) and drops six small
+launches per layer. The micro-benchmark's CUTLASS baseline (452-461 GB/s at M=1) was not autotuned
+(`bench_sm120_flat_mxfp4_moe.py` runs `cutlass_fused_moe` without FlashInfer's `autotune()`; SGLang tunes at warmup,
+`model_executor/runner/base_runner.py:256`), so it understated the production path by ~2x. Root cause of the missing
+gain: at M <= 6 the work decomposition (kTileN = 64, kBK1 = H/4, kBK2 = I/2) yields ~1 item per resident block
+(down: 6 experts x 80 n-tiles x 2 splits = 960 items over 752 blocks), so each block fills its 3-stage pipeline once
+and drains; the persistent grid never reaches streaming steady state. `build_jobs` costs 6.6 us at 224 experts
+(2.1 us at 32). Correctness: `routed_scaling_factor` (1.5) is folded into `topk_weights` by the router
+(`fuse_routed_scaling_factor_in_topk = True`; the CUTLASS runner applies it on output only for bf16/fp8 quant types),
+so the flat path receives scaled weights and is consistent. Verdict: not a swap-in candidate as built. Next step if
+pursued: smaller N tiles / K splits at small M so every block streams several tiles, a job builder that scans only
+the touched experts, and an autotuned CUTLASS baseline in the micro-benchmark. Side finding from the same profile:
+the bf16 LM head (`head.weight` [129280, 5120]) runs as 45 cuBLAS `gemvx` launches per step, 1.59 ms at ~0.4 TB/s;
+a block-FP8 GEMV path for it would save ~1 ms per decode step (8%).
