+# Prune follow-ups: the "padding cliff" and saliency-based keep sets
+
+## A. Expert-count padding: there is none in the weight path (evidence)
+Hypothesis under test: keep-200/208/216/224 all cost ~93 GiB/rank while keep-192
+costs ~80, so the expert allocation is padded (to 256, or to a 32-multiple).
+Every allocation stage was checked; **none pads or aligns the expert count**:
+- `_install_prune` / `create_weights`: the four params are allocated at exactly
+  `num_kept` rows (now from birth, see below). `torch.empty` is exact (caching
+  allocator rounds per tensor to <=2 MiB, <0.1%).
+- `block_scale_interleave` (SM120 scale swizzle): numel preserved at TP shapes
+  for E in {192,200,208,216,224} (measured) -- it swizzles 128-row tiles inside
+  each expert; expert count is untouched.
+- FlashInfer `cutlass_fused_moe`: **runs at E=200, 208, 224, reading exactly E
+  rows** (measured, tiny TP-shaped weights) -- no expert-count alignment
+  requirement. The wrapper takes `num_experts = fc2_expert_weights.shape[0]`.
+- FlashInfer C++ MoE sources (`cutlass_fused_moe_kernels.cuh`, binding,
+  `da_moe.cuh`): grep finds no `round/align` of `num_experts`; the workspace's
+  `num_experts_per_node` terms are small int/pointer histograms
+  (`expert_first_token_offset`, `alpha_scale_ptr_array`); the large terms scale
+  with tokens x hidden/intermediate, not experts.
+- sglang `moe/` + `quantization/`: no expert-count rounding. The NVIDIA fp4
+  block-quant path reads `weight_param.shape[0]`; the intermediate-dim padding
+  (`fp4_k_align=128`, "384 -> 512") is the **AMD aiter** branch; the fnuz/mxfp8
+  re-Parameter branches are ROCm/other quant, same E.
+
+**Conclusion:** keep-224 resident experts cost exactly
+224 x 40 x 8.96 MiB = **80.3 GiB/rank**; + ~12-13 GiB dense/embed/vision =
+the ~93 GiB you measured. That is the true cost of 224 experts, not padding.
+There is no 32/64/128/256 alignment to remove, so keep-208 should genuinely
+land at 208 x 40 x 8.96 = 74.5 + ~12 = **~87 GiB/rank** (your target) and
+keep-200 at ~84. A flat 200..224 plateau cannot come from the weight path;
+it must come from a runtime allocation (kernel workspace, CUDA-graph pool, KV
+sizing) or the measurement itself.
+
+Diagnostic to settle it (one line per boot): log `torch.cuda.memory_allocated()`
+right after "Load weight end" for keep-192/200/208/224. Linear in the keep
+count => weights exact, the step is downstream; flat => something re-allocates
+at load, and the log line pins which stage.
+
+**Change made:** pruned layers are now allocated at `num_kept` from birth --
+`create_weights` passes the kept count to `_fp8.create_weights` instead of
+allocating `[384,...]` and resizing. This removes a 3.6 GB/layer transient and
+the alloc/free churn (a plausible source of allocator fragmentation that could
+inflate reserved memory non-linearly); `_install_prune` now just verifies the
+row count and fails loud.
+
+## B. Saliency-based keep sets (REAP-style proxy)
+Top-K by routing **count** keeps the busy experts; REAP importance
+`sum_t gate_weight(e,t) * ||expert_output(e,t)||` keeps the *useful* ones. The
+fused kernel does not expose output norms, so the stats hook
+(`SGLANG_DSV41_EXPERT_STATS_DIR`, `_record_expert_stats`) now also accumulates
+per expert, in the same `.pt` next to `counts`:
+- `weight_sum` = sum of top-k gate weights (the saliency proxy),
+- `weight_sq`  = sum of squared weights,
+- `weight_max` = largest weight seen.
+Padding/-1 goes to the trailing bin with weight 0. Graph-capture-safe (skipped
+during capture, like the counter). Weights carry the top-k's fused
+routed_scaling_factor -- a constant that leaves rankings intact.
+
+`srt/model_loader/expert_pack/make_keep_set.py` emits the keep-set JSON:
+```
+python3 -m sglang.srt.model_loader.expert_pack.make_keep_set \
+  --stats /path/stats --keep 192 --criterion weight_sum --out keep192_w.json
+#   --criterion {count, weight_sum, weight_sq, weight_max}
+```
+It sums stats across ranks (identical routing under TP: rescale only), drops
+the padding bin, takes top-K per layer, and prints per-criterion coverage
+(worst layer / mean) of the chosen set, so keep-192-by-weight vs -by-count can
+be compared on routing mass before the quality A/B. Old stats files without the
+weight keys fail loud for weight criteria (re-profile) and still work for
+`count`. Verified on a synthetic stats dir where count and weight_sum rankings
+deliberately differ.
+
+## C. DSpark drafter pruning (keep keys `mtp.N`)
+The DSpark drafter (`srt/models/deepseek_v4_dspark.py`, checkpoint tensors
+`mtp.0/1/2.*`) is three `DSparkV4Stage(DeepseekV4DecoderLayer)` stages at module
+prefix `stages.N`, each a `DeepseekV2MoE` with 128 routed experts, top-3, the same
+`noaux_tc` sqrtsoftplus + `e_score_correction_bias` gate (no vision bias: the
+stage config zeroes `vision_n_layers`), the same shared expert, and the same
+`Mxfp4FlashinferCutlassMoEMethod` expert path as the target. So the prune
+mechanism applies unchanged; only the keep-set key had to learn a second form.
+
+Keep-set JSON (`SGLANG_DSV41_EXPERT_KEEP_SET`), one file for both families:
+```
+{"0": [...224 of 384...], ..., "39": [...],       # target model.layers.N
+ "mtp.0": [...64 of 128...], "mtp.1": [...], "mtp.2": [...]}   # drafter stages.N
+```
+`keep_key_of(prefix)` maps `model.layers.N...` -> `N` and `stages.N...` -> `"mtp.N"`;
+`create_weights` (kept-count allocation + loader map), `apply()` (top-k remap),
+the stats hook and the router mask all resolve through the module prefix. Any
+other key fails at load; a set whose ids do not fit the layer (a 384-id set on
+a 128-expert stage) fails when that layer is built. `mtp.*` keys are unused on
+a boot without DSpark.
+
+Fixed alongside: `DeepseekV2MoE` used to key the router mask by `layer_id`, and
+a stage's `layer_id` is its stage index (0..2), so every keep-set + DSpark boot
+masked the drafter's experts down to those ids that happened to be in the
+target's layer-0/1/2 sets (~53 of 128 per stage dropped at keep-224) while its
+weights stayed fully loaded. Drafter acceptance lengths measured under prune
+before this fix carry that handicap.
+
+Profiling + keep set (the drafter routes only inside speculative decode, and
+graph replays never reach the stats hook):
+```
+# eager DSpark boot with stats on
+--speculative-algorithm DSPARK --speculative-dspark-block-size 5 --disable-cuda-graph
+SGLANG_DSV41_EXPERT_STATS_DIR=/stats SGLANG_DSV41_EXPERT_STATS_EVERY=16
+# stats land as /stats/stages.{0,1,2}.mlp.experts.rank{0,1}.pt next to the target's
+python3 -m sglang.srt.model_loader.expert_pack.make_keep_set \
+  --stats /stats --base dsv41-keep224.json --keep-draft 64 --out dsv41-keep224-d64.json
+#   (or --keep 224 --keep-draft 64 to rebuild both families from one profile)
+```
+Sizing (TP2, per rank): a stage's routed experts are 128 x [w1 2304x2560 + w3
+2304x2560 + w2 5120x1152] I8 = 2.11 GiB packed FP4 + 135 MiB E8M0 scales, halved
+by the intermediate-dim TP shard = 1.12 GiB/stage/rank, 3.36 GiB for mtp.0-2.
+Keep 64/128 frees ~1.68 GiB/rank (1.8 GB); keep 96/128 frees ~0.84 GiB/rank.
+The rest of the measured 5.2 GB drafter cost (attention, main_proj, Markov head,
+draft graphs) is untouched. Expect a small accept-length drop; measure it.
+
+Tests (CPU, synthetic weights): `test/srt/models/test_dsv41_dspark_expert_prune.py`
+-- prefix-keyed lookup (no stage/target aliasing), pruned loader map + remap
+bit-identical to the full set restricted to kept experts, masked router == top-k
+over kept only, fail-loud on unknown keys / out-of-range ids / wrong row counts,
+and `make_keep_set --keep-draft/--base` output.
