index 0000000..0d8f0a0
--- /dev/null
+++ b/sglang/NOTES.md
@@ -0,0 +1,164 @@
+# SM120 FP8 block-scaled dense linears: route non-128 N off Triton onto CUTLASS
+
+## Problem
+
+Torch profiler (DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash, 2x RTX PRO 6000 / SM120, 12 decode steps):
+the 2nd-heaviest CUDA cost was the Triton `_w8a8_block_fp8_matmul`
+(`kernels/ops/quantization/fp8_kernel.py:942`) — 406 ms / 2432 calls — used for
+the FP8 block-[128,128] dense/attention linears.
+
+## Root cause
+
+On SM120 the block-FP8 GEMM backend defaults to **CUTLASS**, not Triton:
+`initialize_fp8_gemm_config()` remaps `auto -> cutlass` on SM120
+(`srt/layers/quantization/fp8_utils.py`), so `dispatch_w8a8_block_fp8_linear()`
+returns `cutlass_w8a8_block_fp8_linear_with_fallback`, which calls the SM120
+CUTLASS blockwise kernel `fp8_blockwise_scaled_mm`
+(`kernels/ops/gemm/fp8_blockwise_gemm.py` ->
+`kernels/jit/csrc/gemm/fp8_blockwise/fp8_blockwise_scaled_mm_sm120.cuh`, a real
+SM120 blockwise GEMM with a swap-AB small-M decode path).
+
+That kernel requires **N % 128 == 0**: its entry asserts
+`mat_b.size(1) / 128 == scales_b.size(1)` (floor division), and the Python
+wrapper guarded `weight.shape[0] % 128 == 0 and weight.shape[1] % 128 == 0`,
+falling back to Triton otherwise.
+
+DeepSeek-V4.1 runs `fused_qkv_a_proj_with_mqa` every layer every decode step;
+its output dim is `q_lora_rank + kv_lora_rank + qk_rope_head_dim = 1280 + 512 +
+64 = 1856`, which is **not** a multiple of 128. So that linear — alone among the
+dense linears (`q_b_proj` 6144, `o_proj` 5120, shared-expert 4608/5120 are all
+%128 and already on CUTLASS) — took the Triton fallback on every layer, which is
+the profiler hotspot.
+
+FlashInfer's SM120 blockscaled GEMM (`flashinfer.gemm.gemm_fp8_nt_groupwise` /
+`dense_blockscaled_gemm_sm120_b12x`) has the **same** `tile_n % 128` requirement,
+and `bmm_fp8` is per-tensor (wrong format), so neither is a drop-in for N=1856.
+The reference B12X vLLM lane pads non-128 dims (`b12x.gemm.block_fp8_linear`
+`_align_up`, `deepseek_v4/nvidia/ops/prepare_megamoe.py` pads hidden). Padding N
+to 128 is the established fix.
+
+`SGLANG_OPT_FP8_WO_A_GEMM=False` (set for SM120 in
+`srt/arg_groups/model_hook.py:362`) is a **separate** path: it makes the MLA
+`wo_a` absorb GEMM run in bf16 (`wo_a_bf16_gemv`), not through
+`_w8a8_block_fp8_matmul`, so it does not contribute to this hotspot. Re-enabling
+fp8 `wo_a` needs a 128-block fp8 *batched/einsum* absorb kernel on SM120
+(DeepGEMM has none; the CUTLASS `fp8_blockwise_scaled_mm` is a 2D GEMM, not the
+per-token batched einsum) — out of scope for this hotspot.
+
+## Fix
+
+`srt/layers/quantization/fp8_utils.py`:
+
+- New `_cutlass_block_fp8_pad_weight_n()` (~L1061-1088) + `_CUTLASS_BLOCK_FP8_N_PAD`
+  cache: returns the fp8 weight `[N, K]` zero-padded on N up to a multiple of 128,
+  cached per weight (`id()` key + `weakref.finalize` eviction). Padded rows are
+  fp8 zeros -> their GEMM columns are zero and are sliced off; the real rows keep
+  their bytes and block-128 scale layout, so the first N output columns are
+  bit-identical. `weight_scale` already has `ceil(N/128)` rows = `padded_N/128`,
+  so it is reused unchanged.
+- `cutlass_w8a8_block_fp8_linear_with_fallback()` (~L1090-1154): split the old
+  `shape_supported` into `k_supported` (K % 128, hard -> still Triton) and
+  `n_supported` (N % 128). When only N is unaligned, run CUTLASS on the padded
+  weight and slice the output back to N (`output[:, :n].contiguous()`). The
+  %128 path is byte-for-byte unchanged (no pad, no slice).
+- `import weakref` at top.
+
+This is SM120-scoped because `cutlass_w8a8_block_fp8_linear_with_fallback` is only
+reachable on SM120 (the `cutlass` backend raises elsewhere). It preserves block
+[128,128] ue8m0 numerics and reuses the already-validated CUTLASS kernel on a
+fully-supported padded shape.
+
+## Flag / env to enable
+
+No new flag. It rides the existing default: SM120 `--fp8-gemm-backend auto` (or
+explicit `cutlass`). `--fp8-gemm-backend triton` still forces Triton; other
+backends are unaffected. No model_hook.py change needed — SM120's default is
+already CUTLASS.
+
+## Microbench
+
+`benchmark/bench_sm120_block_fp8_dense.py` — CUDA-graph timed (decode runs
+graph-captured in production, so this isolates device time), RTX PRO 6000 / SM120,
+us per call and CUTLASS GB/s (weight-bytes / time). Correctness vs the Triton path
+is to fp8 precision (`max|Δ|` 0.0000-0.008).
+
+```
+shape                          M  triton_us  cutlass_us  speedup  cutls_GB/s  max|Δ|vsTriton
+fused_qkv_a_proj 5120->1856    1     29.96      13.42     2.23x      708.1      0.0000   <-- target
+fused_qkv_a_proj 5120->1856    4     27.48      13.65     2.01x      696.1      0.0000
+fused_qkv_a_proj 5120->1856   16     27.48      13.70     2.01x      693.6      0.0002
+o_proj          16384->5120    1    104.64      35.40     2.96x     2369.7      0.0020
+shared gate_up   5120->4608    1     39.32      14.26     2.76x     1654.6      0.0000
+shared down      2304->5120    1     24.86       8.74     2.84x     1349.0      0.0002
+q_b_proj         1280->6144    4     10.56       5.63     1.88x     1396.4      0.0005
+```
+
+`fused_qkv_a_proj` (the one that was on Triton) is ~2.2x faster at decode M=1
+(29.96 -> 13.42 us); saved ~15 us/layer x ~61 layers ~= ~0.9 ms per decode step.
+The other rows (already on CUTLASS) confirm the kernel choice: CUTLASS beats
+Triton 1.9-3.0x across the real shapes.
+
+### Running it
+
+```
+CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES=0 PYTORCH_CUDA_ALLOC_CONF=max_split_size_mb:128 \
+  python benchmark/bench_sm120_block_fp8_dense.py
+# via the image (source bind-mounted), GPU pinned, < ~1 GiB:
+docker run --rm --gpus '"device=0"' \
+  -v <src>:/sgl-workspace/sglang/python/sglang:ro \
+  -e PYTHONPATH=/sgl-workspace/sglang/python -e CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES=0 \
+  --entrypoint bash local/sglang-dsv41:base \
+  -c 'cd /sgl-workspace/sglang/python/sglang && python benchmark/bench_sm120_block_fp8_dense.py'
+```
+
+# SM120 decode: block-fp8 GEMV replaces quantize + CUTLASS MXFP8 GEMM at small M
+
+Flag `SGLANG_DSV41_DECODE_DENSE_GEMV=1` (default off), cap
+`SGLANG_DSV41_DECODE_DENSE_GEMV_MAX_M` (default 4; kernel limit 32).
+
+**Problem.** With `--fp8-gemm-backend flashinfer_cutlass` every [32,32]-block ue8m0
+dense linear (`wqkv_a`, `wq_b`, `wo_b`, shared `w13`/`w2`, indexer `wq_b`; 208
+calls/step at TP2) runs `MXFP8QuantizeSwizzledKern` + `DeviceGemmMxfp8GemmSm120`.
+At decode M the GEMM pads M to a 128-row tile and launches N / tile_N CTAs, so it
+streams ~2.8 GB/step of weight at ~290 GB/s (18% of HBM): 9.6 of the 18 ms step.
+
+**Fix.** `kernels/jit/csrc/gemm/sm120_block_fp8_gemv.cuh` (+ `kernels/ops/gemm/
+sm120_block_fp8_gemv.py`): warp-per-row streaming GEMV over the fp8 weight with the
+fp32 `weight_scale_inv` block scales applied per 16-value chunk, bf16 activations
+consumed directly (no quantize launch, ~10x lower error vs fp32 than the MXFP8
+path), masked K tail (K = 1280 / 1152). Dispatched from `Fp8LinearMethod.apply`
+only for layers already on the MXFP8 route (`block_fp8_mxfp8_ready`) and
+M <= MAX_M; prefill and larger M keep CUTLASS bit-for-bit.
+
+**Runtime M, no mid-decode JIT.** The row count is a kernel argument bounded by a
+compile-time bucket (1, 2, 4, 8, 16, 32): one build per (shape, bucket), 36 for
+the model. DSpark produces every M in 1..6*bs (draft-extend sum(accepted+1),
+verify 6*bs), which with a per-M template compiled a new variant (~3.3 s each)
+the first time each M appeared: a 53 s stall observed in the fast lane.
+`process_weights_after_loading` now prebuilds every bucket up to MAX_M per layer
+shape; builds persist in the JIT cache `SGLANG_JIT_CACHE_DIR` (default
+`~/.cache/sglang/jit`, i.e. `/root/.cache/sglang/jit` in the image), which the
+lanes should mount so a cold boot does not repay 36 x 3.3 s.
+
+**Measured** (RTX PRO 6000 Blackwell, CUDA-graph timed, every weight copy touched
+once per graph pass over > 128 MB per shape so nothing is L2-resident;
+`benchmark/bench_sm120_block_fp8_dense.py --m 1,2,4,6,12,24`), us per call:
+
+| shape K->N (per rank) | calls/step | quant+CUTLASS M=1 | GEMV M=1 (GB/s, % of 1.6 TB/s) | GEMV M=6 | M=12 | M=24 (CUTLASS) |
+|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
+| wqkv_a 5120->1792 | 40 | 29.5 | 8.2 (1124, 70%) | 10.5 | 16.8 | 29.6 (27.0) |
+| wq_b 1280->16384 | 40 | 72.8 | 14.5 (1444, 90%) | 17.4 | 28.6 | 52.7 (62.5) |
+| wo_b 4096->5120 | 40 | 71.5 | 14.7 (1423, 89%) | 18.5 | 27.9 | 49.6 (61.9) |
+| sh_w13 5120->2304 | 40 | 39.1 | 9.5 (1248, 78%) | 10.7 | 16.6 | 29.7 (33.8) |
+| sh_w2 1152->5120 | 40 | 22.4 | 5.3 (1110, 69%) | 8.1 | 11.4 | 20.6 (19.7) |
+| idx_wq_b 1280->4096 | 8 | 20.2 | 4.8 (1095, 68%) | 6.6 | 9.5 | 15.8 (17.7) |
+
+max abs err vs fp32 reference (M=1): CUTLASS 0.036-0.076, GEMV 0.0033-0.0077.
+Per step (rank 0), CUTLASS -> GEMV: M=1 9.57 -> 2.13 ms (-7.45), M=2 -7.40,
+M=4 -6.75, M=6 -6.90, M=12 -5.14, M=24 -0.92 (break-even: 24 fp32 FMAs per
+streamed byte). Projection at bs=1: 18 -> ~10.5 ms/step, ~56 -> ~95 tok/s;
+full-model lane A measured 55.5 -> 94.2 tok/s (single), 97 -> 154 at cc2.
+cuDNN MXFP8 (10-33 us) and the Triton fallback (17-314 us) also lose at M <= 12.
+Config sweep (rows {1,2,4} x unroll {1,2,4} x warps {4,8}) was flat within 3%;
+default `(1, 1, 8)`. A tensor-core (mma) variant would be needed to keep the
+win past M ~ 16.
